<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>true</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Every version here is exact. Central package management rejects floating
    ranges outright (NU1011), and that is the right rule: a range makes a red
    build indistinguishable from an upstream release, and it makes today's
    build unreproducible tomorrow.

    W0 still owns admission. These are working pins, not a reviewed dependency
    set: the license, maintenance, release cadence and transitive graph of the
    Windows App SDK and of the Wintun redistributable are still open, and no
    release candidate may ship against them until that review lands.

    The Windows App SDK moved to a component metapackage in 2.x. 2.4.0 is the
    generation contemporary with .NET 10; the 1.7 line predates it. Neither
    line can be compiled from this machine, so this pin is reasoned rather
    than verified, and the Windows CI job is what settles it. If that job
    fails on the SDK rather than on the code, this is the line to reconsider.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.WindowsAppSDK" Version="2.4.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Windows.SDK.BuildTools" Version="10.0.26100.8249" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The test stack never ships, so it carries none of the redistribution
    obligations under review above.

    One package. xunit v3 runs on Microsoft.Testing.Platform and builds the
    test project as its own executable, so it needs neither the VSTest host
    (Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk) nor a separate adapter
    (xunit.runner.visualstudio). Three packages became one, and `dotnet test`
    still drives it.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.v3" Version="3.2.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
